 "why": "The board as it actually stood when the founder counted 59 open rows, and the cluster it was supposed to describe. 60 open P0 rows named 31 objects; 41 objects existed and 4 were not Ready. Kept verbatim because a plan is only proven against the board it will meet, and this one is worse than a synthetic fixture in every way that matters: 53 rows named an object that was Ready at that moment, 2 were duplicates of one still-burning object (jit), 1 named an object deleted from the cluster (flux-webhook), and the live fire of the day (quad-ledger) had no row at all. One P0 in the list is not this alarm's (#5867, the login drill) and must be left alone.\n\nWhy the rows accumulated is measured, not inferred, and is not in this file: the alarm's recovery step closes exactly one row per recovery event (`gh issue list --search 'in:title ...' | head -1` in .github/workflows/flux-events.yml), so an object that fired N times keeps N-1 of its rows open for good. Counted on 2026-10-12: 19 titles held a row closed by a recovery and another row older than that close still open (jit: #5338 closed 2026-10-12T06:58:35Z while #5270 and #4362 stayed open; estate-db: #4823 closed 2026-09-30T21:04:46Z while #2703, #3287 and #3536 stayed open). That is why bin/flux-board-reconcile reads no events at all.",
